   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-precalculus",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.15",
    "topic_title": "Rates of Change in Polar Functions",
    "stem": "A polar function r = f(θ) has f(π/6) = −3 and f(2π/3) = 1. What is the average rate of change of the signed radius r with respect to θ from θ = π/6 to θ = 2π/3?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "8/π",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The average rate of change is [f(2π/3)−f(π/6)]/(2π/3−π/6). The signed radius changes by 1−(−3) = 4, and the angle changes by π/2, so the rate is 4/(π/2) = 8/π."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "−4/π",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Distance-for-radius error: using |r| gives a change of 1−3 = −2 instead of the signed-radius change of 1−(−3) = 4. The question asks about signed radius, not distance from the origin. Fix: Subtract the given radius values without taking absolute values."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "4",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Change-for-rate error: 4 is the change in signed radius, but it does not account for the angle interval. An average rate requires dividing that change by the angle change. Fix: Divide the radius change by 2π/3−π/6."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "π/8",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Reciprocal-rate error: dividing the angle change by the radius change gives (π/2)/4 = π/8. This reverses the requested rate of radius change with respect to angle. Fix: Put the radius change in the numerator and the angle change in the denominator."
     }
    ]
   },
